
# Neighbor Detection – How to Run

This project implements a simple neighbor detection protocol in C using UDP broadcast.  
Below are the minimal steps to compile and simulate three routers on one machine.

# Technique 1: 
## 1. Compile

From the project directory:

```bash
gcc -Wall -Wextra -pthread part1.c -o part1
````

This creates the executable `part1`.

## 2. Set up the virtual routers

The script `setup_routers.sh` creates three Linux network namespaces (r1, r2, r3) and connects them.

```bash
chmod +x setup_routers.sh
./setup_routers.sh
```

## 3. Add broadcast routes

The program sends to `255.255.255.255`. Add a route for this inside each namespace:

```bash
sudo ip netns exec r1 ip route add 255.255.255.255 dev veth-r1
sudo ip netns exec r2 ip route add 255.255.255.255 dev veth-r2
sudo ip netns exec r3 ip route add 255.255.255.255 dev veth-r3
```

## 4. Run the three routers (three terminals)

Open three terminals (or three VS Code terminals), all in the project directory.

Terminal 1:

```bash
sudo ip netns exec r1 ./part1
```

Terminal 2:

```bash
sudo ip netns exec r2 ./part1
```

Terminal 3:

```bash
sudo ip netns exec r3 ./part1
```

Each instance will print its local IP, send HELLO messages, and show discovered neighbors.

## 5. Test neighbor timeout (optional)

Stop one router (for example, the `r3` terminal) with `Ctrl+C`.
After about 10 seconds, the other routers should print that `10.0.0.3` timed out.

## 6. Clean up

When finished, remove the namespaces and virtual network:

```bash
chmod +x cleanup_routers.sh
./cleanup_routers.sh
```

This deletes r1, r2, r3 and the virtual interfaces.

# Technique 2: 
run in the terminal to compile:

```bash
make
```

run in the terminal to run and prepare everything in one command: 

```bash
make run-all
```
to terminal click: ctrl + C

run in the terminal to teardown everything in one command: 

```bash
make teardown
```
```bash
make clean
```

